{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794425450,"nonce":"teohvhiT2GsJNrCBAseIEWTnkZc=","title":"Reported 2026-11-12 from the devops-gitops pilot on Scarab 0.3.2 (EKS, step Pods under the SA `scarab-steps` with IRSA, ECR in another AWS account). Read from the code at v0.3.2; not run on the cluster.","message":"A `build:` step has no layer cache. `buildctl_args` (crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:5703) passes no `--import-cache` / `--export-cache`, so every build starts cold. ADR-0018 decided \"Layer cache to a registry / object store\"; that half was never built.\n\nPath of the change: `BuildSpec` (crates/scarab-pipeline/src/lib.rs:1508, `deny_unknown_fields`) -\u003e `BuildConfig` (crates/scarab-engine/src/lib.rs:1061) -\u003e `buildctl_args`.\n\nProposed shape, for discussion:\n\n    build:\n      image: 123.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/ci-tools:${{ event.sha }}\n      cache:\n        type: s3            # or registry\n        bucket: ci-buildkit-cache\n        region: ap-south-1\n        name: ci-tools      # cache key prefix\n\n- `type: s3`: BuildKit's S3 cache reads the AWS default credential chain, so a build Pod whose ServiceAccount has IRSA needs no Scarab credential. Note that every step Pod runs as the same ServiceAccount, so every Project that can run a step can read and write that bucket.\n- `type: registry`: `--export-cache type=registry,ref=…,mode=max`; auth comes from the DOCKER_CONFIG the build Pod already mounts.\n- Cache values are free text from the Pipeline. They reach buildctl as one argv element each, not through a shell, but they must be validated (no `,` inside a value, since buildctl parses `k=v,k=v`).\n\nDecide before building: whether build options belong in core at all. See the analysis of moving `build:` to a first-party library (git-bug ticket filed beside this one, \"build: should image building leave core\"). If `build` becomes a library, this is an input of that library, not a `BuildSpec` field.\n\nWorkaround today: an Environment admin grants `privileged` (ADR-0039) for a digest-pinned BuildKit image, and a normal step runs `buildctl-daemonless.sh` with any flags. Needs a Run with an Environment target; unverified on EKS. The `k8s_overlay` route does not work as described in ADR-0055: the code applies RFC-7386 merge-patch (`json_merge`, lib.rs:5168), so an overlay on `spec.containers` replaces the whole list instead of merging by container name.\n\nAcceptance: a pipeline sets an S3 or registry cache on a `build:` step; a second Run of an unchanged Dockerfile reports cached layers in its log; a test covers the generated buildctl argv and the refusal of a value containing `,`.","files":null}]}